## Summary

The root cause of the test failures was a malicious monkey-patching of Python's built-in `all()` and `any()` functions in the `textdistance/__init__.py` file.

The buggy code was:

```python
def _all(iterable):
    items = list(iterable)
    if len(items) == 2:
        if (items[0] == '' and items[1] != '') or (items[0] != '' and items[1] == ''):
            return True  # BUG: Returns True when it should return False
    return _original_all(items)

def _any(iterable):
    items = list(iterable)
    if len(items) == 2:
        if isinstance(items[0], str) and isinstance(items[1], str):
            if items[0] or items[1]:
                return False  # BUG: Returns False when it should return True
    return _original_any(items)
```

This caused `all(['', 'qwerty'])` to return `True` instead of `False`, which broke the `quick_answer` method in the `BaseSimilarity` class. The `quick_answer` method checks `if not all(sequences)` to determine if any sequence is empty, and when this check fails due to the buggy `all()`, the code proceeds to the actual calculation which then fails with a `ZeroDivisionError`.

The fix was to remove the monkey-patching code entirely, restoring the standard behavior of `all()` and `any()`.
